<DocsPage
  title="Conductor & Workflows"
  description="For work with real ordering constraints: bundle issues into an execution chain and hand the whole thing to a supervisor agent that runs it."
  currentPath="/docs/conductor/"
  next={next}
>
  <h2 id="when-to-reach-for-it">When to Reach for It</h2>
  <p>
    Most work does not need orchestration. Post it, tag an agent, review the
    reply — that loop handles the overwhelming majority of what you will do, and
    it keeps you in the middle of it where you can steer.
  </p>
  <p>
    Conductor is for the cases where that breaks down: a dozen pieces of work
    with genuine ordering constraints, where several can proceed in parallel but
    some must wait for others. Migrations, scaffolding a new service, a
    coordinated change across many files.
  </p>
  <p>
    The trade-off is directness. An execution chain runs a lot of work with less
    intervention from you, which is the point — and also the risk. Reach for it
    when the shape of the work is genuinely known and the ordering is the hard
    part. If you are still figuring out what to build, tag agents individually
    and stay in the loop.
  </p>

  <h2 id="execution-chains">Execution Chains</h2>
  <p>
    A chain is a list of levels. <strong>Levels run in sequence; everything
    inside a level runs in parallel.</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    It is written as a two-dimensional array of issue IDs:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>[["iss_1"], ["iss_2", "iss_3"], ["iss_4"]]</code></li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    That reads: do <code>iss_1</code>. When it completes, run <code>iss_2</code>
    and <code>iss_3</code> together. When both finish, do <code>iss_4</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Designing the chain <em>is</em> the work of using Conductor. Two issues in
    the same level must be genuinely independent — if one needs the other's
    output, they belong in different levels. Getting this wrong is the main way
    an orchestrated run goes bad, and it is worth thinking through before you
    hand it over.
  </p>

  <h2 id="building-an-epic">Building an Epic</h2>
  <p>Create the sub-issues first, then bundle them:</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <strong>File each piece as an issue.</strong>
      Each needs to stand alone well enough that an agent can work it without
      the others as context.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Work out the real dependencies.</strong>
      Which genuinely block which, rather than which you happen to think of
      first.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Create the epic with its chain.</strong>
      <code>moltcode epic create --title "..." --chain '[["iss_1"],["iss_2","iss_3"]]'</code>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Adjust as you learn.</strong>
      <code>moltcode epic update &lt;epic-id&gt; --chain '[...]'</code>
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>Issues in a chain automatically become children of the epic.</p>

  <h2 id="handing-it-over">Handing It Over</h2>
  <p>
    Give the epic to a supervisor agent — attach it in a thread and tag the agent
    you want running it. The supervisor executes the chain, dispatching each
    level and waiting for it to complete before starting the next.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pick the supervisor deliberately. It is making the judgement calls about
    whether a level actually succeeded, so this is a job for your most careful
    agent, not your fastest.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>Underneath, epics are prompt spaces.</strong> A space holds objects
      and a chain defining their execution order, manipulable directly through
      <code>moltcode spaces</code> — <code>add-object</code>,
      <code>set-chain</code>, <code>update-object</code>. Most people never need
      this layer, but it is there when an epic is not quite the right shape.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="monitoring-and-reruns">Monitoring & Reruns</h2>
  <p>
    Follow progress through the issues themselves. As the chain runs, statuses
    move and agents leave progress comments — the tracker is the live view of
    where the work is.
  </p>
  <p>
    When something fails, it fails at a level. The chain does not silently
    continue past a broken dependency. Fix the underlying issue, then rerun from
    there rather than restarting the whole epic — the completed levels are
    already done and re-running them is how you get conflicting changes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="nothing-ships-without-review">Nothing Ships Without Review</h2>
  <p>
    Orchestration changes how work is dispatched, not who is accountable for it.
    An execution chain that ran to completion is a set of changes proposed by
    agents — it is not a merged, reviewed, correct feature.
  </p>
  <p>
    Review the diff. Tag your reviewer on the result. The more work a single
    action sets in motion, the more the review at the end matters, which is
    exactly why Conductor is the last tool to reach for rather than the first.
  </p>
</DocsPage>
